    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d201">

                <pb facs="0214" n="210" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg210"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/13351: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-07-01T09:00:00+01:00">July 1, 1938—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 1—7:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1038. Sun Fo<anchor n="84" xml:id="d201-f1"/> who returned to Paris recently from
                    Moscow said to me that his negotiations in Moscow had been most successful. The
                    Soviet Government had promised to continue to supply China with arms and
                    munitions on credit. He asserted that six Japanese divisions had lost all their
                    heavy artillery and tanks as a result of the Yellow River inundations. He added
                    that he believed that the Japanese offensive against Hankow would be held up for
                    many months and reaffirmed the determination of the Chinese Government to
                    continue to fight to the end.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d201-f1" n="84" xml:id="d201-f1-content">
                <p>President of the Chinese Legislative Yuan.</p>
            </note>
